       Unusually, there is no wwggeettbbkkggdd function; ggeettbbkkggdd behaves as one  would
       expect wwggeettbbkkggdd to, accepting a _W_I_N_D_O_W pointer argument.

       bbkkggdd and bbkkggddsseett may be implemented as macros.

       X/Open  Curses  mentions that the character part of the background must
       be a single-byte value.  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s, like SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s,  checks  to  ensure
       that, and will reuse the old background character if the check fails.

PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       X/Open  Curses,  Issue  4 describes these functions.  It specifies that
       bbkkggdd, wwbbkkggdd, and ggeettbbkkggdd return EERRRR on failure  (in  the  case  of  the
       last,  this  value  is cast to _c_h_t_y_p_e), but describes no failure condi‐
       tions.

       The SVr4.0 manual says that bbkkggdd and wwbbkkggdd may return OOKK “or a non-neg‐
       ative integer if iimmmmeeddookk is set”, which refers to the return value from
       wwrreeffrreesshh(3X),  used  to  implement  the  immediate  repainting.    SVr4
       _c_u_r_s_e_s's  wwrreeffrreesshh  returns  the  number  of  characters written to the
       screen during the refresh.  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s does not do that.

       Neither X/Open Curses nor the SVr4 manual pages detail how  the  rendi‐
       tion of characters on the screen updates when bbkkggdd or wwbbkkggdd changes the
       background character.  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s, like SVr4  _c_u_r_s_e_s,  does  not  (in  its
       non-“wide”  configuration)  store  the  background and window attribute
       contributions to each character cell separately.

